 dict(slug='industrial-inspection', title='Industrial visual inspection', kind='Personal research · 2026', category='vision research', tags='Python, PyTorch, CUDA, Anomaly detection', summary='From a statistical baseline to a PatchCore variant: making small PCB defects visible, and measuring what fails.', problem='Small defects can disappear when a full board image is resized. I am exploring normal-only learning for inspection, with explicit trade-offs between missed defects and false alarms.', contribution='I built a Python CLI spanning data preparation, training, threshold calibration, prediction, evaluation and benchmarking. The implementation includes a NumPy reference and a GPU PatchCore variant with frozen Wide ResNet-50-2 features.', approach='Deterministic manifests, duplicate screening and frozen artifacts support reproducible comparisons. I compared full-frame 256 px inputs with board crops at 256 and 512 px, using normal-only calibration for each configuration.', result='On the PCB2 development split, cropped 512 px input found 43 of 50 defects and flagged 1 of 50 normal images. Full-frame 256 px found 36 defects and flagged 3 normals. Median decode-plus-inference time increased from 22.8 to 38.5 ms.', limitation='These are custom development experiments, not an official benchmark or independent acceptance test. PCB1/PCB2 physical independence is unestablished; near-duplicate candidates need review. Heatmaps are qualitative. On the low-resolution Innodisk set, the 512 px configuration achieved AUROC 0.5023 and flagged 3 of only 4 normal images. Generalization remains unresolved.', source=LINKEDIN, source_label='Discuss the research on LinkedIn', status='Active research · source private', diagram='inspection'),
 dict(slug='landslide-segmentation', title='Mapping landslides from multispectral imagery', kind='Collaborative academic project · 2023', category='vision research', tags='Semantic segmentation, UNet, Swin, SegFormer', summary='Comparing convolutional and transformer models for a small, highly imbalanced class in satellite imagery.', problem='Landslide pixels form a small minority in satellite images. A segmentation model must distinguish them from a large background class.', contribution='In a TU Braunschweig team project with Reiko Lettmoden, we compared UNet, a Swin-based UNet and SegFormer, studying input bands, augmentation and loss functions.', approach='The Landslide4Sense inputs combine multispectral and terrain information. We used a subset of the original training data with a local split, and compared cross-entropy and Lovász-based training.', result='The report records F1 0.756 for augmented UNet with Lovász loss, versus 0.721 for baseline UNet: a 3.5 percentage-point difference in our project experiments.', limitation='Team results, not an individual benchmark claim. Experiments used 50% of the original training data and a custom split; scores are not official challenge test results.', source='https://deep-learning-igp-tubs-sose2023.github.io/Group_C/', source_label='Read the team report', status='Completed academic collaboration', diagram='satellite'),
 dict(slug='physics-informed-networks', title='Learning with physical constraints', kind='Master’s thesis · 2025', category='research', tags='TensorFlow, PINNs, PDEs, Hydra', summary='Investigating how training choices affect neural solutions to Helmholtz and Allen–Cahn equations.', problem='Physics-informed neural networks combine boundary conditions with differential-equation residuals. Their training behavior depends on how those constraints are represented and optimized.', contribution='For my master’s work, I implemented and committed PINN experiment scripts and saved solution, residual and error outputs, using TensorDiffEq and pinns-tf2 as existing frameworks.', approach='The experiments explore Helmholtz and Allen–Cahn problems, collocation strategies, adaptive weighting and optimizer choices. The repository contains configurations, training logs and solution visualizations.', result='The work produced an experiment collection for examining neural PDE solutions and their residuals. It connects deep learning with numerical methods and scientific computing.', limitation='I do not claim authorship of the underlying PINN frameworks. A consistent final comparison has not been established for this portfolio, so no numerical improvement is presented.', source='https://github.com/muecke-math/Master-Amit-Amit', source_label='Explore the thesis repository', status='M.Sc. thesis · completed June 2025', diagram='pinn'),
 dict(slug='character-chat', title='A full-stack character AI playground', kind='Personal application · 2025', category='engineering', tags='Next.js, TypeScript, Express, MongoDB, OpenRouter', summary='Connecting a character interface, authenticated API and hosted language models in one application.', problem='An interactive character application needs to connect browsing, saved characters and generated conversation across frontend and backend boundaries.', contribution='I built a Rick and Morty character application with a Next.js/TypeScript frontend and an Express backend, integrating the public character API and OpenRouter-hosted language models.', approach='The repository includes MongoDB/Mongoose models, JWT authentication, character search, customization and chat. The chat route validates inputs, constructs persona prompts and handles upstream errors.', result='The source demonstrates full-stack AI integration, including character backstories and conversational features. Local setup instructions are available in the repository.', limitation='This uses hosted models; it is not model-training research. No production reliability, security audit or measured quality claim is made. A live demo has not been verified.', source='https://github.com/ak06645/rick-and-morty-ai-app', source_label='Explore the application code', status='Source available · local setup', diagram='app'),
